WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s crazy when you think about all of
the different kinds of foods we eat.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.760
We just swallow and hope
it all works out for the best.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Well, as it turns out,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
there are better ways to think about keeping
our bodies humming healthfully along.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.800
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.680
In this episode,

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.800
Dr. Kristine Dennis continues
a deep dive into the research

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.040
around raising
plant based kids.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.840
Dr. Dennis is our new
Senior Research Scientist

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.120
who also happens to
be a plant-based parent.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.800
Today, Dr. Dennis asks

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.160
how can we plan well-balanced plant-
based diets for infants and kids?

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.640
The American Academy
of Nutrition and Dietetics

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.280
considers plant-based diets
suitable for pregnancy,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.480
as well as during lactation,
infancy, and childhood,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.720
when they are well-planned.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.000
So, what goes into planning

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.760
a well-balanced
plant-based diet

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.360
for infants and kids?

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.480
For infants, breast milk
is the basis of nutrition,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.480
consisting not only
of critical nutrients

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.320
but also
immune-boosting compounds

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.760
providing both short
and long-term benefits.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.080
The World Health Organization
recommends breastfeeding

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.320
exclusively for the first
six months of life,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.840
and continuing
with the introduction

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.520
of complementary foods.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.200
What do we know about
the breast milk composition

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.280
of plant-based moms?

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.440
Vegetarian and vegan mothers
provide breast milk

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.120
with comparable
nutritional value,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.600
with a couple important caveats.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.240
In a direct comparison
of breast milk from omnivore

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.320
and vegetarian or vegan moms,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.560
the notable difference
was lower DHA,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.560
the long chain omega-3,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.480
in the milk
from plant-based moms,

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.680
due to their avoiding fish,
which is probably a good thing

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.960
given how contaminated
the aquatic food chain is.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.960
Thankfully,
ensuring proper supplementation

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.000
with pollutant-free sources
can bridge this gap,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.920
just like when there is
proper supplementation

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.760
with vitamin B12.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.440
So, provided that women
are eating enough

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.200
to cover their
nutritional requirements

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.880
and supplementing
with DHA and vitamin B12,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.520
vegan moms can produce
nutritionally similar milk.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.720
But what about
case reports like this

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.072
where an infant on a vegan diet
is failing to thrive?

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.120
These case reports highlight
the importance of good nutrition

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.240
during this period of life
of extremely rapid growth.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.000
By around six months of age,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.240
solid foods
should be introduced.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.480
By that age,
breast milk or formula alone

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.440
is not sufficient

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.680
to meet the nutritional
needs of infants.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.880
In the first case study,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.560
the infant
was exclusively breastfed

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.400
for 10 months,
and then was given

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.440
a nutritionally insufficient
rice-based drink.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.960
In the second case report,

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.080
the infant did not receive
vitamin D supplementation,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.120
which is recommended for all
children in early life.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.880
Breast milk
should be continued on demand

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.680
until the child
is at least one year old.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.560
Formula is also an option
for women who cannot breastfeed.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:43.920
The VegPlate Junior,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.160
a guide put together
to help with dietary planning

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.760
for plant-based kids,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.240
has a helpful visual,
and a more detailed table,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.280
for infants who have started
consuming complementary foods.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.920
And finally,
as we've highlighted before,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.560
raw diets are not appropriate
to meet the nutritional needs

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.920
of infants and children.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.520
So, what about nutrient status
and growth in vegan children?

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.120
First, caution must be applied

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.440
to some populations
defined as vegan,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.640
but who may follow diets
that refuse supplementation.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.760
This is where you can get the
impression from some experts

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.160
that vegan diets are
deficient and harmful.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.680
But the current
literature suggests

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.480
that plant-based diets
using supplementation

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.480
are likely to provide
the recommended amounts

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.800
of critical nutrients
to allow normal progression

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.640
of height and weight
in children,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.880
and can be beneficial.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.720
There is a reason
to assume that vegans today

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.640
may be planning their diets
more adequately

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.320
than vegans in the past,

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.080
as this way of eating
has been adopted

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.920
in the general population,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.720
rather than existing
solely in fringe groups.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.760
And, lucky for us,
there is a new set of studies

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.840
aimed at tackling
this very question.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.320
What are the associations
between the modern practice

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.760
of vegetarian and vegan diets

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.040
in children and
adolescents and health?

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.680
Enter the VeChi study,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.520
the Vegetarian and Vegan
Children Study of German kids.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.960
For the one to three-year-olds

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.440
eating plant-based
in the VeChi study,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.120
how did their growth compare
to their omnivorous peers?

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.240
Overall, vegan, vegetarian,
and omnivorous kids

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.760
did not significantly differ.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.680
This is in line
with other studies.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.040
One to three-year-old veg kids

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.800
tend to be slightly
smaller and lighter

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.480
(but within the normal range)

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.160
or have no differences
on average

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.840
compared to omni kids.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.720
When the researchers assessed
micronutrients and fatty acids,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.000
they noted special focus

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
should be paid
to critical nutrients

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.080
like vitamin D, iodine,
and DHA for all children,

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.520
regardless of their diet,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.520
with extra attention
on riboflavin,

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.520
vitamin B12, calcium,
and iron for veg kids.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.280
For a toddler, intake
of riboflavin (vitamin B2)

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.680
should be around
500 micrograms per day.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.880
The veg kids were short
by about 40-70 mcg.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.720
Luckily, there are
an abundant variety

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.960
of plant-based sources
like nuts, legumes,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.200
and fortified plant-
based milks.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.040
For example,
if your toddler drinks

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.880
a cup (240 ml)
of fortified soy milk,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.480
they have already met the
500 mcg recommended intake.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.120
For calcium,
great plant sources include

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.320
dark green vegetables,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.200
(like kale, bok choy,
and broccoli),

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.480
tofu, almonds,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.880
and calcium-fortified
non-dairy beverages,

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.080
like soy milk.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.000
What about the older
VeChi kids, aged 6 to 18?

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.880
Although iron intake was low
in the toddler group,

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.360
it was higher
in the older veg children,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.280
surpassing the omnivore kids.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.400
There was no elevated risk for
iron deficiency in the veg kids.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.480
The higher intake of iron
in the veg group

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:33.760
may have helped compensate

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.280
for the lower bioavailability
of iron from plant foods.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.400
The kids in this group

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.040
were also more likely
to take supplements.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.951
Yet, in another recent study,

00:05:42.951 --> 00:05:44.640
this one of 5 to 10
year old Polish kids

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.640
with adequate
reported iron intakes,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.360
about 8 percent
had mild or moderate

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:50.960
hemoglobin deficiency.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.760
Regardless of diet,
about 3 percent

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.760
of one to two-year-olds
in the United States

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.000
have iron deficiency anemia,

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.560
and given its consequences
for young children,

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.720
prevention is a top priority.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.800
For adolescent
and young adult females,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.760
iron deficiency anemia
is around 6 percent,

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.080
and menstruation
increases this risk.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.600
It's worth
paying special attention

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.160
to include iron-rich foods
at most meals

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.560
and, to give a little boost
of iron absorption,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.880
combine with
vitamin C rich foods.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.600
What else did we learn
about the older VeChi kids?

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.080
BMI did not differ
across groups.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:25.680
They were a pretty
healthy bunch.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.120
The plant-based kids had been
veg for four to five years

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.440
and most vegan kids
took supplements,

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.120
in particular B12,
as they absolutely should.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.520
Hopefully,
the 12 percent who weren't

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.880
were eating sufficient amounts
of B12-fortified foods,

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.040
as well as vitamin D.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.920
One way the groups did differ
was that the vegan kids

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.400
had significantly lower
LDL cholesterol

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.360
compared to the vegetarian
and omnivorous kids.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.200
And given that atherosclerosis,

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.000
our number one killer,
starts in childhood,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.800
and dietary habits
track into adulthood,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.160
plant-based diets in childhood

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.960
could ultimately improve the
cardiometabolic health of adults,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.360
as well as potentially
help prevent obesity,

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.040
which often persists
into adulthood.

